    pr_ref:  # 1353
    description: "Scope-expansion companion to WI-guhok. Filed during WI-guhok investigation 2026-04-22.\n\nContext:\nThe io_primitives YAML catalog declares attribute-style primitives (module + attributes: [environ, argv, ...]) in 6 languages: python, javascript, java, c, go, rust. The IoBoundaryCatalog loader parses them into IoPrimitive(kind='attribute') objects. But NO analyzer currently emits edges for attribute-read expressions like 'os.environ[\"HOME\"]' — only call-expressions produce edges. Consequence: attribute-primitive entries in every YAML catalog are dead metadata across every language.\n\nWI-guhok (this item's parent) ships the Python-only fix: py.py will emit 'module_attr_ref' edges for imported-module attribute reads, and io_boundary.py's tag_io_boundaries will include that edge type. That unblocks the hypergumbo self-analysis audit and any Python-only downstream use case.\n\nThis follow-up item covers the 12 tree-sitter-based languages that still need the same treatment (js_ts, java, c, cpp, csharp, go, kotlin, objc, php, ruby, rust, scala, swift).\n\nProposed design — base class helper:\nAdd TreeSitterAnalyzer._emit_module_attribute_refs(tree, imports, caller_symbol, node_kinds, extract_base_attr) to analyze/base.py. Walks the tree for nodes matching node_kinds (supplied per language), resolves each node's 'base' against imports, and emits a 'module_attr_ref' edge when the base is an imported module. The per-language config is small:\n  - JavaScript/TypeScript: node_kind='member_expression' ; extract via .child_by_field_name('object') and .child_by_field_name('property')\n  - Java: node_kind='field_access' ; extract via similar accessor\n  - Go: node_kind='selector_expression' ; extract via .child_by_field_name('operand') and '.field'\n  - C: node_kind='field_expression' ; similar\n  - Rust: node_kind='field_expression' ; similar\n  - etc. for the remaining tree-sitter langs\n\nEach language analyzer calls self._emit_module_attribute_refs(...) from its existing edge-extraction pass. One-line call-site addition per analyzer plus the per-lang config tuple.\n\nValidation:\nFor each language, add a probe fixture under tests/fixtures/ exercising at least one attribute-style primitive from that language's YAML (e.g. process.env.PATH in JS; System.out.println('x') — ipc_send in Java). Test that tag_io_boundaries produces a chain with the right primitive name. Then re-run hypergumbo self-analysis on hypergumbo itself + on a representative JS/Java/Go repo and confirm env_read / ipc_send chain_count increases meaningfully.\n\nArchitectural note (investigation finding worth recording):\nOn 2026-04-22 I initially mis-reported the TreeSitterAnalyzer inheritor count as 13-of-42, which would have been a notable architectural gap. Correct numbers:\n  - 32 of 42 analyzer files in hypergumbo-lang-mainstream inherit from TreeSitterAnalyzer\n  - 105 total inheritors across hypergumbo-lang-{mainstream,common,extended1}\nThe base class is in fact widely inherited; the non-inheritors divide cleanly into: (a) the standalone code analyzers that use non-tree-sitter backends (py.py uses ast, rust_scip.py uses SCIP, *_def_use.py uses SCIP for DDG), and (b) config/manifest scanners where there's no real AST to walk (bash.py, toml_config.py, ini.py, gitignore.py, requirements.py, jvm_deps.py, json_config.py, xml_config.py, yaml_ansible.py, etc.). If the design benefit of pushing attribute-ref emission into the base class holds, it covers ~105 of the ~119 claimed supported languages — plenty. But: worth a quick check at implementation time of whether every tree-sitter subclass actually calls the shared helper from its edge-extraction pass, or whether some subclasses have their own bespoke AST walks that would bypass a base-class hook. That would be an analogous structural smell to the one WI-guhok uncovered.\n\nBlockers and sequencing:\nDepends on WI-guhok being merged first, because WI-guhok adds the 'module_attr_ref' edge type to io_boundary.py's call_types and _TRACEABLE_EDGE_TYPES sets. Once that lands, this item is additive per-language and can be split into per-language sub-PRs (one per language) if that's easier than one big PR."  # 1353
    fields: {}  # 1353
